{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EajD8r6eU1V8M3enXsnRbS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WI-227 — Run-menu trailing arguments: decide the contract, then quote it (repo-review-2026-07-18 M-06)\n2\t\n3\t**Branch:** dualplan-routing-fix · **Filed:** 2026-07-18 (review M-06) ·\n4\t**BuildTier:** medium · **SafetyClass:** ordinary (launcher convenience\n5\tlayer; not the dispatcher).\n6\t\n7\t## Why\n8\t\n9\t`run_menu.py:116` concatenates trailing user arguments into a shell command\n10\tstring unquoted. An argument containing spaces or shell metacharacters\n11\tsplits or executes differently than the user intended, and Windows\n12\t(`cmd.exe`) vs POSIX quoting rules differ, so a naive quoting patch is\n13\tunsafe without deciding what the contract *is*. The review deliberately left\n14\tthis unfixed pending that decision (§0 table).\n15\t\n16\t## Scope\n17\t\n18\t1. **Decide the contract** (recorded in this spec as a ruling section,\n19\t   WI-223-style): either trailing arguments are a **trusted shell fragment**\n20\t   (documented as such — the user owns quoting; the kit passes it through\n21\t   verbatim on both platforms) or they are **data arguments** (the kit\n22\t   appends them as argv items with per-platform quoting, never re-parsed by\n23\t   a shell). The recommendation is data-arguments: menu entries are the\n24\t   trusted recipes; user-supplied trailing args are values.\n25\t2. **Implement** the ruled semantics explicitly — argv-append or documented\n26\t   passthrough — with tests covering spaces, quotes, and `&`/`|`\n27\t   metacharacters on both Windows and POSIX (skip-guarded like the suite's\n28\t   existing shell-dependent tests).\n29\t\n30\t**Non-goals:** no menu-format redesign; no change to how the menu's own\n31\trecipe lines execute.\n32\t\n33\t## Ruling — data arguments (2026-07-18)\n34\t\n35\tOption (2) is ratified: **trailing arguments are data values, not shell text.**\n36\tThe declared `[run]` recipe stays the trusted shell line (`shell=True`, pipes,\n37\t`&&`, redirects — unchanged); each trailing arg the launcher forwards\n38\t(`run_menu.py <name> arg…`, the old `exec $RUN_CMD \"$@\"`) is appended *quoted for\n39\tthe platform shell* so it reaches the program as exactly one literal token —\n40\tnever re-split on whitespace and never re-parsed as a shell operator. POSIX uses\n41\t`shlex.quote`; Windows wraps each token in double quotes with the\n42\tcmd.exe/MSVCRT backslash-quote escaping, which makes separators and redirects\n43\t(`&` `|` `<` `>` `(` `)` `^`) inside the quotes literal.\n44\t\n45\tData-arguments is right because the menu recipe is the trusted recipe and the\n46\tlauncher's `\"$@\"` is a value an evaluator types (a path with spaces, a filter\n47\tcontaining `&`); a value must not silently become part of the command grammar.\n48\tPassthrough-verbatim would instead make every trailing arg a shell injection the\n49\tuser has to hand-quote differently per platform — the exact cross-platform trap\n50\tthe review flagged.\n51\t\n52\tTwo Windows-only residual limits remain, rooted in cmd.exe's own parser rather\n53\tthan fixable by more escaping, and are therefore **documented, not fought**\n54\t(SafetyClass is ordinary — a convenience layer): (a) a `%VAR%` in a value is\n55\tstill expanded, because a single `cmd /c` command string cannot suppress cmd.exe\n56\tenvironment expansion; (b) a literal double quote *combined with* a separator in\n57\tone value can re-expose that separator, the known cmd.exe-vs-MSVCRT quote-state\n58\tdisagreement. POSIX (`shlex.quote`) has neither — it neutralizes `$`, backticks,\n59\tand every metacharacter totally. Both limits are recorded in the `run_menu.py`\n60\tmodule docstring, the home an adopter reads.\n61\t\n62\t**Non-goals (ruling):** no attempt at a perfect cmd.exe universal-quoter; no\n63\tchange to how the recipe lines themselves execute.\n64\t\n65\t## Done-when\n66\t\n67\tThe ruling recorded; behavior implemented per ruling with cross-platform\n68\tregression tests proven failing pre-fix under the ruled semantics; smoke bar\n69\tgreen per commit, full suite at close.\n70\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8129915d-af74-417e-9484-18e89a7f2f16","uuid":"be7a346c-bd52-48eb-9524-f531ee026237","timestamp":"2026-07-18T22:29:29.860Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\1-g3-WI-227-cec3\\docs\\specs\\WI-227.md","content":"# WI-227 — Run-menu trailing arguments: decide the contract, then quote it (repo-review-2026-07-18 M-06)\n\n**Branch:** dualplan-routing-fix · **Filed:** 2026-07-18 (review M-06) ·\n**BuildTier:** medium · **SafetyClass:** ordinary (launcher convenience\nlayer; not the dispatcher).\n\n## Why\n\n`run_menu.py:116` concatenates trailing user arguments into a shell command\nstring unquoted. An argument containing spaces or shell metacharacters\nsplits or executes differently than the user intended, and Windows\n(`cmd.exe`) vs POSIX quoting rules differ, so a naive quoting patch is\nunsafe without deciding what the contract *is*. The review deliberately left\nthis unfixed pending that decision (§0 table).\n\n## Scope\n\n1. **Decide the contract** (recorded in this spec as a ruling section,\n   WI-223-style): either trailing arguments are a **trusted shell fragment**\n   (documented as such — the user owns quoting; the kit passes it through\n   verbatim on both platforms) or they are **data arguments** (the kit\n   appends them as argv items with per-platform quoting, never re-parsed by\n   a shell). The recommendation is data-arguments: menu entries are the\n   trusted recipes; user-supplied trailing args are values.\n2. **Implement** the ruled semantics explicitly — argv-append or documented\n   passthrough — with tests covering spaces, quotes, and `&`/`|`\n   metacharacters on both Windows and POSIX (skip-guarded like the suite's\n   existing shell-dependent tests).\n\n**Non-goals:** no menu-format redesign; no change to how the menu's own\nrecipe lines execute.\n\n## Ruling — data arguments (2026-07-18)\n\nOption (2) is ratified: **trailing arguments are data values, not shell text.**\nThe declared `[run]` recipe stays the trusted shell line (`shell=True`, pipes,\n`&&`, redirects — unchanged); each trailing arg the launcher forwards\n(`run_menu.py <name> arg…`, the old `exec $RUN_CMD \"$@\"`) is appended *quoted for\nthe platform shell* so it reaches the program as exactly one literal token —\nnever re-split on whitespace and never re-parsed as a shell operator. POSIX uses\n`shlex.quote`; Windows wraps each token in double quotes with the\ncmd.exe/MSVCRT backslash-quote escaping, which makes separators and redirects\n(`&` `|` `<` `>` `(` `)` `^`) inside the quotes literal.\n\nData-arguments is right because the menu recipe is the trusted recipe and the\nlauncher's `\"$@\"` is a value an evaluator types (a path with spaces, a filter\ncontaining `&`); a value must not silently become part of the command grammar.\nPassthrough-verbatim would instead make every trailing arg a shell injection the\nuser has to hand-quote differently per platform — the exact cross-platform trap\nthe review flagged.\n\nTwo Windows-only residual limits remain, rooted in cmd.exe's own parser rather\nthan fixable by more escaping, and are therefore **documented, not fought**\n(SafetyClass is ordinary — a convenience layer): (a) a `%VAR%` in a value is\nstill expanded, because a single `cmd /c` command string cannot suppress cmd.exe\nenvironment expansion; (b) a literal double quote *combined with* a separator in\none value can re-expose that separator, the known cmd.exe-vs-MSVCRT quote-state\ndisagreement. POSIX (`shlex.quote`) has neither — it neutralizes `$`, backticks,\nand every metacharacter totally. Both limits are recorded in the `run_menu.py`\nmodule docstring, the home an adopter reads.\n\n**Non-goals (ruling):** no attempt at a perfect cmd.exe universal-quoter; no\nchange to how the recipe lines themselves execute.\n\n## Done-when\n\nThe ruling recorded; behavior implemented per ruling with cross-platform\nregression tests proven failing pre-fix under the ruled semantics; smoke bar\ngreen per commit, full suite at close.\n","numLines":70,"startLine":1,"totalLines":70}}}
