{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018rCNifgfbFmQXR8C1kyYAy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"The run capability menu — one launcher that presents every major capability.\n3\t\n4\tThe root `run.cmd` / `run.sh` / `run.command` launchers are thin delegates to\n5\tthis script (process.md §7, \"the evaluator's rungs\"): running the product must\n6\tnever depend on recalling a command. Capabilities are **declared once** in\n7\t`docs/stack.ini`'s `[run]` section and read here, so the launch command lives in\n8\texactly one place instead of being duplicated across the platform launchers.\n9\t\n10\t`docs/stack.ini` `[run]` section format:\n11\t\n12\t    [run]\n13\t    serve = docker compose up -d && python -m webbrowser http://localhost:8080\n14\t    serve.desc = start the app and open its page\n15\t    iso = ./build_iso.sh\n16\t    iso.desc = build the bootable ISO and launch the burner\n17\t\n18\tOne `<name> = <command>` line per capability (in declaration order), plus an\n19\toptional `<name>.desc = <one line>`. Each command is a full **shell line** — a\n20\tmulti-step capability keeps its steps in a project script and names it here once.\n21\t\n22\tUsage (what the launchers invoke as `run_menu.py \"$@\"`):\n23\t    run_menu.py            no args  -> a numbered interactive menu (pick one)\n24\t    run_menu.py <name>     direct launch of that capability (exit code passes through)\n25\t    run_menu.py --list     stable machine-readable listing (the agent surface)\n26\t\n27\t`--list` prints one capability per line as `name<TAB>desc` (desc empty when none),\n28\tin declaration order — a parse-stable contract for agents/scripts; it exits 0 with\n29\tempty output when nothing is declared. The interactive and direct paths instead\n30\tprint the same \"no run capability wired yet\" guidance and exit 1 on an absent or\n31\tempty `[run]` section, exactly as the pre-menu launchers did — a pure library\n32\tsimply deletes the `run.*` launchers.\n33\t\n34\tStdlib-only, Python 3.8+, Windows + POSIX (the same portability contract as the\n35\trest of the kit). The command is run with `shell=True`: the value is the user's\n36\town declared shell line from their own `docs/stack.ini` (the same trust boundary\n37\tas the `RUN_CMD` this replaced — the user edits their own file), and a capability\n38\tis deliberately a full shell command (pipes, `&&`, redirects), so it is handed to\n39\tthe shell verbatim rather than split into argv.\n40\t\n41\t**Trailing arguments** the launchers forward (`run_menu.py <name> arg…`, the old\n42\t`exec $RUN_CMD \"$@\"`) are treated as **data values, not shell text** (the WI-227\n43\truling): the recipe is trusted, an evaluator's `\"$@\"` is a value. Each is\n44\tappended to the recipe *quoted for the platform shell* (`_quote_extra`) so a\n45\tvalue with spaces, quotes, or an `&`/`|` reaches the program as one literal\n46\targument rather than splitting or executing. POSIX uses `shlex.quote` (total).\n47\tTwo Windows-only limits are documented rather than fought (this is an ordinary\n48\tconvenience layer, not the dispatcher): cmd.exe still expands a `%VAR%` in a\n49\tvalue (a single `cmd /c` line cannot suppress it), and a literal double quote\n50\tcombined with a separator in one value can re-expose that separator (the\n51\tcmd.exe-vs-MSVCRT quote-state disagreement). The recipe lines themselves stay\n52\ttrusted shell text.\n53\t\n54\tContracts: IF-048, IF-049 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n55\t\"\"\"\n56\t\n57\timport argparse\n58\timport configparser\n59\timport os\n60\timport shlex\n61\timport subprocess\n62\timport sys\n63\tfrom pathlib import Path\n64\t\n65\t# The declared toolchain, read at repo root when present (the launchers cd there\n66\t# first). Same file check.py reads; this script only ever touches its [run]\n67\t# section, which check.py ignores.\n68\tDEFAULT_STACK_INI = Path(\"docs/stack.ini\")\n69\t\n70\t\n71\tdef _utf8_console():\n72\t    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n73\t    non-ASCII capability name / desc / command banner can't raise\n74\t    UnicodeEncodeError on a legacy Windows cp1252 console (the sibling scripts'\n75\t    guard). Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`; guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n76\t    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n77\t        try:\n78\t            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n79\t        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n80\t            pass\n81\t\n82\t\n83\tdef load_capabilities(path):\n84\t    \"\"\"Parse the `[run]` section of a stack.ini into an ordered list of\n85\t    (name, command, desc). Returns [] when the file or section is absent/empty.\n86\t\n87\t    Declaration order is preserved (configparser is insertion-ordered), key case\n88\t    is preserved (a capability may be `Serve`), and `interpolation=None` keeps a\n89\t    literal `%`/`{}` in a command untouched. A malformed profile fails LOUDLY —\n90\t    a typo can't quietly hide a capability. A `<name>.desc` with no matching\n91\t    `<name> =` command, or a command with an empty value, is ignored.\"\"\"\n92\t    if not path.exists():\n93\t        return []\n94\t    cp = configparser.ConfigParser(interpolation=None)\n95\t    cp.optionxform = str  # preserve capability-name case\n96\t    try:\n97\t        cp.read_string(path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"), source=str(path))\n98\t    except configparser.Error as exc:\n99\t        sys.exit(\"run_menu: {} is malformed: {}\".format(path, exc))\n100\t    if not cp.has_section(\"run\"):\n101\t        return []\n102\t    descs = {}\n103\t    commands = []\n104\t    for key, val in cp.items(\"run\"):\n105\t        if key.endswith(\".desc\"):\n106\t            descs[key[: -len(\".desc\")]] = val.strip()\n107\t        else:\n108\t            commands.append((key, val.strip()))\n109\t    return [(name, cmd, descs.get(name, \"\")) for name, cmd in commands if cmd]\n110\t\n111\t\n112\tNO_CAPABILITIES = (\n113\t    \"run_menu: no run capabilities declared yet.\\n\"\n114\t    \"Declare them in docs/stack.ini under a [run] section, one line each:\\n\"\n115\t    \"  [run]\\n\"\n116\t    \"  serve = python -m yourapp\\n\"\n117\t    \"  serve.desc = start the app\\n\"\n118\t    \"The README 'Run it' section documents the underlying commands.\\n\"\n119\t    \"(A pure library has none — delete the run.* launchers.)\"\n120\t)\n121\t\n122\t\n123\tdef _win_quote(arg):\n124\t    \"\"\"Quote one trailing DATA argument for a cmd.exe command line (Windows,\n125\t    shell=True). Always wrapped in double quotes so cmd.exe treats separators and\n126\t    redirects (`&` `|` `<` `>` `(` `)` `^`) inside as literal text, with the\n127\t    MSVCRT backslash/quote escaping the target program's argv parser expects:\n128\t    each run of backslashes preceding a `\"` is doubled and the `\"` escaped, and a\n129\t    trailing run before the closing quote is doubled too. `%VAR%` expansion and a\n130\t    quote-plus-separator value are the documented residual limits (module\n131\t    docstring).\"\"\"\n132\t    out = ['\"']\n133\t    backslashes = 0\n134\t    for ch in arg:\n135\t        if ch == \"\\\\\":\n136\t            backslashes += 1\n137\t            out.append(ch)\n138\t            continue\n139\t        if ch == '\"':\n140\t            out.append(\"\\\\\" * backslashes)  # double the run already emitted\n141\t            out.append('\\\\\"')\n142\t        else:\n143\t            out.append(ch)\n144\t        backslashes = 0\n145\t    out.append(\"\\\\\" * backslashes)  # double a trailing run before the close quote\n146\t    out.append('\"')\n147\t    return \"\".join(out)\n148\t\n149\t\n150\tdef _quote_extra(extra):\n151\t    \"\"\"Quote trailing DATA arguments so the shell line receives each as exactly\n152\t    one literal token — the WI-227 data-argument contract (module docstring\n153\t    'Trailing arguments'). POSIX uses shlex.quote (total); Windows uses the\n154\t    cmd.exe/MSVCRT quoting above.\"\"\"\n155\t    quote = _win_quote if os.name == \"nt\" else shlex.quote\n156\t    return \" \".join(quote(a) for a in extra)\n157\t\n158\t\n159\tdef launch(command, extra):\n160\t    \"\"\"Run a capability's shell line, returning its exit code (passthrough).\n161\t\n162\t    The declared command is the trusted shell recipe; `extra` (trailing args the\n163\t    launcher forwarded, the old `exec $RUN_CMD \"$@\"`) are DATA values, each quoted\n164\t    per-platform (`_quote_extra`) so the shell can't re-split a value on\n165\t    whitespace or a metacharacter. shell=True is intentional for the recipe — see\n166\t    the module docstring.\"\"\"\n167\t    full = command if not extra else command + \" \" + _quote_extra(extra)\n168\t    print(\"Running: {}\".format(full), flush=True)\n169\t    return subprocess.run(full, shell=True).returncode\n170\t\n171\t\n172\tdef direct(capabilities, name, extra):\n173\t    \"\"\"Launch the named capability, or report the valid names and exit 2.\"\"\"\n174\t    for cap_name, command, _desc in capabilities:\n175\t        if cap_name == name:\n176\t            return launch(command, extra)\n177\t    print(\n178\t        \"run_menu: no capability named {!r}. Declared: {}\".format(\n179\t            name, \", \".join(n for n, _, _ in capabilities) or \"(none)\"\n180\t        ),\n181\t        file=sys.stderr,\n182\t    )\n183\t    return 2\n184\t\n185\t\n186\tdef interactive_menu(capabilities):\n187\t    \"\"\"Present the numbered menu, read a pick from stdin, launch it.\n188\t\n189\t    Reads from stdin (a TTY when double-clicked, a piped selection otherwise);\n190\t    an EOF (closed/exhausted stdin) ends the loop rather than hanging, so an\n191\t    unattended invocation is safe. `q`/`quit`/blank quits with exit 0.\"\"\"\n192\t    print(\"Run — available capabilities:\\n\")\n193\t    for i, (name, _command, desc) in enumerate(capabilities, 1):\n194\t        line = \"  {}) {}\".format(i, name)\n195\t        if desc:\n196\t            line += \" — \" + desc\n197\t        print(line)\n198\t    print()\n199\t    while True:\n200\t        try:\n201\t            raw = input(\"Select [1-{}] (q to quit): \".format(len(capabilities)))\n202\t        except EOFError:\n203\t            print(\"run_menu: no selection (input closed).\", file=sys.stderr)\n204\t            return 1\n205\t        raw = raw.strip()\n206\t        if raw.lower() in (\"\", \"q\", \"quit\"):\n207\t            return 0\n208\t        try:\n209\t            choice = int(raw)\n210\t        except ValueError:\n211\t            choice = 0\n212\t        if 1 <= choice <= len(capabilities):\n213\t            _name, command, _desc = capabilities[choice - 1]\n214\t            return launch(command, [])\n215\t        print(\"  not a valid choice; enter 1-{} or q.\".format(len(capabilities)))\n216\t\n217\t\n218\tdef main(argv=None):\n219\t    _utf8_console()\n220\t    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(\n221\t        description=\"Present/launch the capabilities declared in docs/stack.ini [run].\",\n222\t        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,\n223\t    )\n224\t    ap.add_argument(\n225\t        \"--list\",\n226\t        action=\"store_true\",\n227\t        help=\"print a stable machine listing (name<TAB>desc, one per line) and exit 0\",\n228\t    )\n229\t    ap.add_argument(\n230\t        \"--file\",\n231\t        default=str(DEFAULT_STACK_INI),\n232\t        help=\"stack.ini to read the [run] section from (default: docs/stack.ini)\",\n233\t    )\n234\t    ap.add_argument(\n235\t        \"name\",\n236\t        nargs=\"?\",\n237\t        help=\"a capability name to launch directly (no name = the interactive menu)\",\n238\t    )\n239\t    ap.add_argument(\n240\t        \"args\",\n241\t        nargs=argparse.REMAINDER,\n242\t        help=\"trailing args forwarded to the launched command\",\n243\t    )\n244\t    ns = ap.parse_args(argv)\n245\t\n246\t    capabilities = load_capabilities(Path(ns.file))\n247\t\n248\t    if ns.list:\n249\t        # The agent surface: parse-stable, empty when nothing is declared (an\n250\t        # agent reads zero lines rather than guidance prose on stdout).\n251\t        for name, _command, desc in capabilities:\n252\t            print(\"{}\\t{}\".format(name, desc))\n253\t        return 0\n254\t\n255\t    if not capabilities:\n256\t        print(NO_CAPABILITIES, file=sys.stderr)\n257\t        return 1\n258\t\n259\t    if ns.name is not None:\n260\t        return direct(capabilities, ns.name, ns.args)\n261\t    return interactive_menu(capabilities)\n262\t\n263\t\n264\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n265\t    sys.exit(main())\n266\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8129915d-af74-417e-9484-18e89a7f2f16","uuid":"742d7235-137e-4c3e-84de-660a7f262e46","timestamp":"2026-07-18T22:29:30.860Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template-trains\\1-g3-WI-227-cec3\\project-trajectory\\scripts\\run_menu.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"The run capability menu — one launcher that presents every major capability.\n\nThe root `run.cmd` / `run.sh` / `run.command` launchers are thin delegates to\nthis script (process.md §7, \"the evaluator's rungs\"): running the product must\nnever depend on recalling a command. Capabilities are **declared once** in\n`docs/stack.ini`'s `[run]` section and read here, so the launch command lives in\nexactly one place instead of being duplicated across the platform launchers.\n\n`docs/stack.ini` `[run]` section format:\n\n    [run]\n    serve = docker compose up -d && python -m webbrowser http://localhost:8080\n    serve.desc = start the app and open its page\n    iso = ./build_iso.sh\n    iso.desc = build the bootable ISO and launch the burner\n\nOne `<name> = <command>` line per capability (in declaration order), plus an\noptional `<name>.desc = <one line>`. Each command is a full **shell line** — a\nmulti-step capability keeps its steps in a project script and names it here once.\n\nUsage (what the launchers invoke as `run_menu.py \"$@\"`):\n    run_menu.py            no args  -> a numbered interactive menu (pick one)\n    run_menu.py <name>     direct launch of that capability (exit code passes through)\n    run_menu.py --list     stable machine-readable listing (the agent surface)\n\n`--list` prints one capability per line as `name<TAB>desc` (desc empty when none),\nin declaration order — a parse-stable contract for agents/scripts; it exits 0 with\nempty output when nothing is declared. The interactive and direct paths instead\nprint the same \"no run capability wired yet\" guidance and exit 1 on an absent or\nempty `[run]` section, exactly as the pre-menu launchers did — a pure library\nsimply deletes the `run.*` launchers.\n\nStdlib-only, Python 3.8+, Windows + POSIX (the same portability contract as the\nrest of the kit). The command is run with `shell=True`: the value is the user's\nown declared shell line from their own `docs/stack.ini` (the same trust boundary\nas the `RUN_CMD` this replaced — the user edits their own file), and a capability\nis deliberately a full shell command (pipes, `&&`, redirects), so it is handed to\nthe shell verbatim rather than split into argv.\n\n**Trailing arguments** the launchers forward (`run_menu.py <name> arg…`, the old\n`exec $RUN_CMD \"$@\"`) are treated as **data values, not shell text** (the WI-227\nruling): the recipe is trusted, an evaluator's `\"$@\"` is a value. Each is\nappended to the recipe *quoted for the platform shell* (`_quote_extra`) so a\nvalue with spaces, quotes, or an `&`/`|` reaches the program as one literal\nargument rather than splitting or executing. POSIX uses `shlex.quote` (total).\nTwo Windows-only limits are documented rather than fought (this is an ordinary\nconvenience layer, not the dispatcher): cmd.exe still expands a `%VAR%` in a\nvalue (a single `cmd /c` line cannot suppress it), and a literal double quote\ncombined with a separator in one value can re-expose that separator (the\ncmd.exe-vs-MSVCRT quote-state disagreement). The recipe lines themselves stay\ntrusted shell text.\n\nContracts: IF-048, IF-049 — the interface seams this module declares (process.md §8; rows of record in docs/requirements/interfaces.csv).\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport configparser\nimport os\nimport shlex\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\n# The declared toolchain, read at repo root when present (the launchers cd there\n# first). Same file check.py reads; this script only ever touches its [run]\n# section, which check.py ignores.\nDEFAULT_STACK_INI = Path(\"docs/stack.ini\")\n\n\ndef _utf8_console():\n    \"\"\"Emit UTF-8 to stdout/stderr whatever the OS console codepage is, so a\n    non-ASCII capability name / desc / command banner can't raise\n    UnicodeEncodeError on a legacy Windows cp1252 console (the sibling scripts'\n    guard). Python 3.7+ streams expose `.reconfigure`; guard for the rest.\"\"\"\n    for s in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):\n        try:\n            s.reconfigure(encoding=\"utf-8\")\n        except (AttributeError, ValueError):\n            pass\n\n\ndef load_capabilities(path):\n    \"\"\"Parse the `[run]` section of a stack.ini into an ordered list of\n    (name, command, desc). Returns [] when the file or section is absent/empty.\n\n    Declaration order is preserved (configparser is insertion-ordered), key case\n    is preserved (a capability may be `Serve`), and `interpolation=None` keeps a\n    literal `%`/`{}` in a command untouched. A malformed profile fails LOUDLY —\n    a typo can't quietly hide a capability. A `<name>.desc` with no matching\n    `<name> =` command, or a command with an empty val
[... byte cap hit — full stream in out/run-logs/ ...]
